# IEEE Paper Writing Guide — CyberRange Project

> This guide shows you **what to write** in each section of your IEEE paper. Fill in your name, college, and actual results.

---

## Suggested Title

> **CyberRange: Design and Implementation of a Web-Based Interactive Cybersecurity Training Platform for Vulnerability Assessment Education**

---

## Keywords (IEEEkeywords)

```
cybersecurity education, ethical hacking, SQL injection, cross-site scripting,
IDOR, OWASP, web security, hands-on training, CTF, vulnerability simulation
```

---

## Abstract (~150 words)

Write something like:

> The growing demand for cybersecurity professionals has highlighted a critical gap between theoretical knowledge and practical skills in academic curricula. This paper presents **CyberRange**, a web-based interactive training platform designed to provide students with hands-on exposure to real-world web application vulnerabilities. The platform simulates intentionally vulnerable environments based on OWASP Top 10 categories, including SQL Injection, Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR), Broken Authentication, and Security Misconfiguration. Each module presents a guided lab scenario where users actively exploit vulnerabilities and receive immediate feedback. Built using a full-stack architecture (React, Node.js, SQLite), the system employs a modular lab registry with isolated database environments to safely simulate attacks without compromising platform integrity. Evaluation results demonstrate improved student engagement and comprehension compared to lecture-only instruction. CyberRange provides an accessible, self-hosted alternative to commercial platforms for cybersecurity education in academic institutions.

---

## Section I — Introduction

**Cover these points:**
- Cybersecurity threats are rising globally (cite a stat — e.g., IBM Cost of Data Breach Report)
- Traditional CS education focuses on theory, not hands-on practice
- Platforms like HackTheBox and TryHackMe exist but are not tailored for structured academic curricula
- **Research gap**: No open-source, self-hostable, academic-focused platform with guided labs + progress tracking
- **Your contribution**: CyberRange fills this gap — a full-stack platform with 5 OWASP-based labs, isolated vulnerable environments, and a progress-tracking dashboard
- Paper structure: "Section II reviews related work. Section III describes system architecture..."

---

## Section II — Related Work

Mention and **compare** to existing platforms:

| Platform | Limitation vs. Your Work |
|---|---|
| DVWA (Damn Vulnerable Web App) | No frontend UI, no guided briefings, no progress tracking |
| HackTheBox / TryHackMe | Commercial, subscription-based, not self-hostable by colleges |
| WebGoat (OWASP) | No modular lab registry, no student dashboard |
| CTF competitions | Event-based, not curriculum-aligned |

**Your advantage**: CyberRange is open, academic-first, guided, and tracks progress per user.

> Cite 2–3 IEEE papers on cybersecurity education or CTF platforms (search IEEE Xplore for "cybersecurity education platform" or "hands-on hacking training").

---

## Section III — System Design & Architecture

### A. Architecture Overview
- 3-tier: React frontend → Express REST API → SQLite databases
- Dual-database design: [platform.db](file:///c:/Users/DURGA%20PRASAD/OneDrive/Desktop/cyberRange/finalYearProject/database/platform.db) (secure) vs [labs.db](file:///c:/Users/DURGA%20PRASAD/OneDrive/Desktop/cyberRange/finalYearProject/database/labs.db) (intentionally vulnerable)
- Explain WHY this separation matters (isolation = real attacks don't affect real accounts)

### B. Modular Lab Registry
- Explain the auto-discovery system ([registerLabs()](file:///c:/Users/DURGA%20PRASAD/OneDrive/Desktop/cyberRange/finalYearProject/server/src/labs/index.js#5-25))
- Any folder added to `/labs/` with a `routes.js` is auto-mounted
- Makes the platform extensible without touching core server code

### C. Database Architecture
```
platform.db  → Secure (parameterized queries, real user auth)
labs.db      → Vulnerable (string concatenation, no sanitization — intentional)
playground.db→ Free-roam (Juice-Shop style simulation)
```

### D. Include an Architecture Diagram
Draw a simple block diagram:
```
[React Client] ←→ [Express Server :4000]
                        ↓
        ┌───────────────┼──────────────────┐
   [platform.db]   [labs.db]        [playground.db]
   (Secure Auth)  (Vulnerable)      (Experimental)
```
Put this as **Fig. 1** in your paper.

---

## Section IV — Implementation

### A. Lab Modules
Describe each lab briefly:

| Lab | Vulnerability | Technique |
|---|---|---|
| Lab 01 | SQL Injection | `' OR 1=1 --` bypasses parameterized-less query |
| Lab 02 | Reflected XSS | `<script>alert(1)</script>` reflected in page |
| Lab 03 | Broken Authentication | Weak token/session logic exploitation |
| Lab 04 | Security Misconfiguration | Exposed debug endpoints |
| Lab 05 | IDOR | Accessing `/documents?userId=2` to view others' files |

### B. Show a Code Snippet (Vulnerable vs Secure)

**Vulnerable (Lab 01 backend):**
```js
// String concatenation — vulnerable to SQL injection
const query = `SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = '${username}' AND password = '${password}'`;
```

**Secure (Platform login):**
```js
// Parameterized query — injection-safe
const query = `SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = ? AND password = ?`;
db.get(query, [username, password], callback);
```
> This side-by-side comparison is a great figure/table for your paper.

### C. Progress Tracking
- `ProgressContext` tracks completed labs
- Score calculated as [(completed / total) * 100](file:///c:/Users/DURGA%20PRASAD/OneDrive/Desktop/cyberRange/finalYearProject/client/src/App.jsx#35-77)
- Dashboard shows live stats

---

## Section V — Results & Evaluation

This is where most students struggle. Here's what **you can do**:

### Option A — User Study (Best)
- Give 10–15 classmates access to the platform
- Record: time to complete each lab, success rate, pre/post quiz scores
- Present as a **table** or **bar chart**

Example table:

| Lab | Avg. Completion Time | Success Rate |
|---|---|---|
| Lab 01 SQLi | 8 min | 90% |
| Lab 02 XSS | 12 min | 80% |
| Lab 05 IDOR | 18 min | 70% |

### Option B — Comparative Analysis (Easier)
Compare CyberRange feature-by-feature vs. DVWA, WebGoat, TryHackMe:

| Feature | CyberRange | DVWA | WebGoat | TryHackMe |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Guided Briefing | ✅ | ❌ | ✅ | ✅ |
| Progress Tracking | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | ✅ |
| Self-Hostable | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ |
| Isolated DB | ✅ | ❌ | ❌ | N/A |
| Free/Open Source | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ❌ |

---

## Section VI — Conclusion

> CyberRange demonstrates that hands-on vulnerability simulation can be effectively integrated into academic cybersecurity education through a modular, self-hosted web platform. The system successfully isolates vulnerable environments from secure platform infrastructure using a multi-database architecture, enabling safe real-world exploit practice. Future work includes adding more OWASP categories (CSRF, File Upload vulnerabilities), implementing an admin dashboard for instructors to monitor student progress, and containerizing the platform using Docker for one-click deployment in institutional environments.

